---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import KineticEnergy from '@/components/tools/calc2/KineticEnergy.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="kinetic-energy-calculator">
  <KineticEnergy client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How the kinetic energy calculator works</h2>
    <p>
      Kinetic energy is the energy a body has because it moves. It grows in proportion to the mass and
      to the square of the speed. The kinetic energy calculator converts your units to kilograms and
      metres per second, applies the formula, and can also solve it for the mass or the speed.
    </p>
    <pre><code>KE = ½ m v²
m  = 2 KE ÷ v²
v  = √(2 KE ÷ m)
1 kWh = 3,600,000 J      1 kcal = 4,184 J</code></pre>

    <h2>Worked example</h2>
    <p>
      A 1,000 kg car at 20 m/s (72 km/h) has KE = 0.5 × 1,000 × 20² = 200,000 J = 200 kJ. At 40 m/s the
      same car has 800 kJ: twice the speed, four times the energy. The brakes must turn all of it into
      heat to stop the car, which is why stopping distance grows much faster than speed.
    </p>
    <p>
      A 145 g baseball at 40 m/s has 0.5 × 0.145 × 40² = 116 J.
    </p>

    <h2>The drawing</h2>
    <p>
      The curve plots energy against speed for the mass you entered. The ink dot is your value; the grey
      dot is twice the speed, at four times the energy. The block at the top moves at a speed in
      proportion to yours, scaled down to fit the screen.
    </p>

    <h2>Limits</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>The formula is for motion in a straight line. A spinning wheel also has rotational energy, ½ I ω².</li>
      <li>It ignores relativity, which matters only near the speed of light.</li>
      <li>The drop height comparison ignores air resistance.</li>
    </ul>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
